    "title": "Jack Nicklaus' son Jackie returns to caddie at Masters Honorary Starter ceremony",
    "text": "Updated April 9, 2026, 10:09 a.m. ETJack Nicklaus, 86, participated in the Honorary Starter ceremony with his son, Jackie, as his caddie.Nicklaus's tee shot went low and left, causing some onlookers to duck.Fellow Honorary Starters Gary Player and Tom Watson joined Nicklaus on the tee box.Jackie Nicklaus previously caddied for his father's 1986 Masters victory.Another moment.Forty years after departing No. 18 green with dad’s bag over his shoulder, Jackie Nicklaus returned to duty for Thursday’s Honorary Starter ceremony.As rows of patrons applauded his father, Jackie, dressed in a white jumpsuit, leaned over and teed up the ball.“That’s why I brought back my caddie. To tee it up for me,” said the Golden Bear, now 86 years old.Jack’s drive agreed to lift off, albeit low and left, causing a line of onlookers to duck.“I did not want to kill anybody,” laughed Jack, as he walked to fellow Honorary Starters, Gary Player and Tom Watson.“You got it airborne,” Watson replied.“Barely,” Nicklaus said.Watson then took his turn and noticed a white tee still planted in the Georgia dirt.“Can I use your tee?” Watson asked.“That’s why I left it,” Nicklaus said.Watson took a practice swing before blasting a drive down No. 1 fairway.The 1977 and 1981 Masters winner jokingly took four steps down the tee box before turning to Jack and Gary. “Let’s play!” Watson said.Similar to 1986, Jack and Jackie then left the course together, the son following behind his father. As the duo reached Augusta National’s clubhouse, Jackie paused and said, “He shared ’86 with me and shared today with me, too.”",
